{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NC391MkdLrdYHXgv5hK8kx","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about diskless object-store operations consistency.\\\\n\\\\n## Answer\\\\n\\\\n**The `list` operation was agreed to remain eventually consistent.**\\\\n\\\\nThis is documented in the discussion thread about KIP-1163 (Diskless Core). In the email from Viktor Somogyi-Vass dated April 24, 2026, he clarifies the consistency requirements:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"Yes, your expectation is totally reasonable, we should expect the get and put operations to be strongly consistent for the read-after-write scenarios... I can expect though that \\\\\\\"list\\\\\\\" can stay eventually consistent as the KIP relies on it for only garbage collection where it is fine if a few segments can be collected only in the next iteration.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nThe agreement was that:\\\\n- **put** and **get** operations should be **strongly consistent** (for read-after-write scenarios)\\\\n- **list** operation can remain **eventually consistent** (since it's only used for garbage collection, where delayed collection is acceptable)\\\\n\\\\n**Files cited:**\\\\n- `_intake/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md` (Viktor Somogyi-Vass's response on April 24, 2026)\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/20/mail-00dfdc315465260213bf900e-b627a53c.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/21/mail-88e93198e095dbab41373878-b88d024a.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/24/mail-9fd9164f86d9ca01e49ed6e7-cab13524.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/27/mail-3b03034816281c500f3c3b46-f0d6e998.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":17.3,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":38462,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":48159,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.10679319999999999,\\\"input_tokens\\\":34,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1319}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"953a4f58-a7ad-43db-80e8-781ee1c45b1f","uuid":"60fbeabd-fe59-4ed3-b1ab-afe78945b1c3","timestamp":"2026-09-27T18:55:36.655Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about diskless object-store operations consistency.\\\\n\\\\n## Answer\\\\n\\\\n**The `list` operation was agreed to remain eventually consistent.**\\\\n\\\\nThis is documented in the discussion thread about KIP-1163 (Diskless Core). In the email from Viktor Somogyi-Vass dated April 24, 2026, he clarifies the consistency requirements:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"Yes, your expectation is totally reasonable, we should expect the get and put operations to be strongly consistent for the read-after-write scenarios... I can expect though that \\\\\\\"list\\\\\\\" can stay eventually consistent as the KIP relies on it for only garbage collection where it is fine if a few segments can be collected only in the next iteration.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nThe agreement was that:\\\\n- **put** and **get** operations should be **strongly consistent** (for read-after-write scenarios)\\\\n- **list** operation can remain **eventually consistent** (since it's only used for garbage collection, where delayed collection is acceptable)\\\\n\\\\n**Files cited:**\\\\n- `_intake/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md` (Viktor Somogyi-Vass's response on April 24, 2026)\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/20/mail-00dfdc315465260213bf900e-b627a53c.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/21/mail-88e93198e095dbab41373878-b88d024a.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/24/mail-9fd9164f86d9ca01e49ed6e7-cab13524.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/04/27/mail-3b03034816281c500f3c3b46-f0d6e998.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":17.3,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":38462,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":48159,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.10679319999999999,\\\"input_tokens\\\":34,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1319}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about diskless object-store operations consistency.\\n\\n## Answer\\n\\n**The `list` operation was agreed to remain eventually consistent.**\\n\\nThis is documented in the discussion thread about KIP-1163 (Diskless Core). In the email from Viktor Somogyi-Vass dated April 24, 2026, he clarifies the consistency requirements:\\n\\n> \\\"Yes, your expectation is totally reasonable, we should expect the get and put operations to be strongly consistent for the read-after-write scenarios... I can expect though that \\\"list\\\" can stay eventually consistent as the KIP relies on it for only garbage collection where it is fine if a few segments can be collected only in the next iteration.\\\"\\n\\nThe agreement was that:\\n- **put** and **get** operations should be **strongly consistent** (for read-after-write scenarios)\\n- **list** operation can remain **eventually consistent** (since it's only used for garbage collection, where delayed collection is acceptable)\\n\\n**Files cited:**\\n- `_intake/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md` (Viktor Somogyi-Vass's response on April 24, 2026)\",\"error\":null,\"files\":[\"store\",\"_intake/2026/04/20/mail-00dfdc315465260213bf900e-b627a53c.md\",\"_intake/2026/04/21/mail-88e93198e095dbab41373878-b88d024a.md\",\"_intake/2026/04/24/mail-9fd9164f86d9ca01e49ed6e7-cab13524.md\",\"_intake/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md\",\"_intake/2026/04/27/mail-3b03034816281c500f3c3b46-f0d6e998.md\"],\"seconds\":17.3,\"truncated\":false,\"turns\":2,\"usage\":{\"cache_read_tokens\":38462,\"cache_write_tokens\":48159,\"cost_usd\":0.10679319999999999,\"input_tokens\":34,\"output_tokens\":1319}}"}}}
